WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.360
 Hello, Marvelites.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.130
You are watching
"This Week in Marvel."

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.210
I'm Ryan a.k.a.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.370
Agent M, and I'm so
excited to be sitting here

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.830
with Dan DeLeeuw, VFX supervisor
of Marvel Studios' "Avengers:

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.630
Endgame."

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.580
Dan, there's so many
amazing moments and shots

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.310
inspired by the
comics here in Marvel

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.640
Studios' "Avengers: Endgame."

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.600
I was looking at
SECRET WARS # 4, like,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.180
the classic mid-'80s issue.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.770
It had this-- when
you were younger,

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:34.910
like, that's impossible!

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.470
The Hulk's holding
up a giant mountain.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.790
He's saving everybody.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:43.144
But then you have that moment
in "Endgame," which was so cool.

00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:44.060
 Rhodey!

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.627
 You're going through,
and you're looking back

00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:47.630
at your old-- your favorite
comics and just kind of, you

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.850
know, taking those
ideas, in "Civil War,"

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.700
with Tony and Cap, you know.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.610
When I start a film, it'll be,
you know, with a team, again,

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.160
with the previous team, I'll
find panels that I like.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.890
And then let's make a folder.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.640
And it's like, OK, this
is one of your shots.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.350
This is one of your shots.
This is one of your shots.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.090
And then eventually, you know,
they don't shoot it that way.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.170
Sometimes you're like,
OK, we're going to go

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:06.970
back and change that shot.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.370
We're gonna change it
back into the panel.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.210
 There's, of course, Captain
America wielding Mjolnir.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:18.627
 Avengers, assemble.

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:20.360
 That has happened a couple
of times in the comics.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.920
But when did you find out that
that was going to be a shot

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.940
you guys get to put together?

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.650
 When you saw it
in Ultron, you know,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.590
when he just kind of, you
know, half picks it up.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.190
And you know, the debate
is whether he's worthy,

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.140
he's not worthy yet or--
it's one of the first things

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.310
we started pre-visiting, right?

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.940
Share your favorite
scenes first,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.262
and you kind of go nuts, right?

00:01:35.262 --> 00:01:36.495
They want something
that's, you know,

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.362
two-minutes long, three-minutes
long, five-minutes long.

00:01:38.362 --> 00:01:40.170
You do something
that's 20-minutes long.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.060
You pull up-- you pull
the best beats out of it.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.000
And does it look
good in the film.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.590
 You have years and
years of history.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.780
But there's some really
more recent moments,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.550
the hail Hydra
moment from "Captain

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.630
America, Steve Rogers."

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.170
That was right before
"Secret Empire."

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:53.970
 Trust me.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.470
Hail Hydra.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.750
RYAN PENAGOS: Or even
Cap fighting Cap,

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.000
which has happened multiple
times in the comics.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.260
But recently that
happened in SECRET EMPIRE.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.060
DAN DELEEUW: Mm-hmm.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.570
RYAN PENAGOS: Or seeing
Rescue, like, amazing moment

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.680
with her and Tony.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.920
But that's only from
INVINCIBLE IRON MAN, which

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.400
is within the last 10 years.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.990
Are there specific areas
you're looking at you?

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.970
Or do you look at any
bit of Marvel history?

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.440
 You do a deep dive, you know.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.340
And then you just kind of--

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.860
you find the pieces that are
going to play the best for you.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.480
And I mean, I think
that's, you know--

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.680
that's-- that's the point.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.150
You look at these
films, and you look

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.540
at for a multitude of reasons
why these are successful.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.760
But they're successful because
you love the characters,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.020
and you love the
comics, and kind

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.160
of bringing in just
those moments for fans

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.780
when they're going
to recognize it.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.870
And again, it's the best part.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.600
 Dan, part of the
journey is the end.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:44.957
And that's the end
of this episode

00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:45.790
of "This Week at Marvel."

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.070
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.940
And everybody watching
and listening,

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.280
make sure to pick up
Marvel Studios' "Avengers:

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.030
Endgame" on digital now.

